# Numbers are split in two on purpose. A JSON number is one syntactic category,
# but collapsing it onto Float alone loses integers above 2^53 — and the values
# that actually flow through JSON in this range (ids, money in minor units,
# epoch millis) are exactly the ones nobody may round. So an integer literal
# becomes JInt and everything else JNum, decided by the literal's *text*
# (parser.number_of), not by a double round-trip.
#
# What this model still cannot hold exactly: integers wider than 64 bits, and
# arbitrary decimal fractions. Integer overflow is rejected. A literal with a
# fraction or exponent is rounded to the nearest IEEE-754 binary64 value with
# ties to even; finite underflow is accepted as signed zero, while overflow to a
# non-finite value is rejected. Exact decimal arithmetic would need a
# decimal/BigInteger case here, which remains a deliberate non-goal.
## The JSON data model (RFC 8259): null, booleans, numbers, strings, arrays and
## objects, which preserve insertion order via the builtin Map. A number is
## `JInt` when its literal carries no '.', 'e' or 'E', and `JNum` otherwise.
pub type Json =
  | JNull
  | JBool(value: Bool)
  | JNum(value: Float)
# An integer-valued number: renders without a decimal point, and is what the
# parser produces for any literal with no '.', 'e' or 'E'.
  | JInt(value: Int)
  | JStr(value: String)
  | JArr(items: List[Json])
  | JObj(entries: Map[String, Json])
derive Show

# Why a parse failed, as something a caller can branch on.
#
# `parse` used to answer `Result[Json, String]`, whose only reader is a human:
# to point at the offending character a caller had to parse the offset back out
# of the sentence, and to distinguish "the document is cut short" from "the
# document is wrong" (the difference between waiting for more bytes and
# rejecting the request), it had to match on prose this package never promised
# to keep stable.
#
# The kinds are the distinctions a caller can *act* on, not one per message:
#
#   Truncated    the input ran out mid-value. An incremental reader asks for
#                more; anything else reports a bad document.
#   Trailing     a complete value, then something after it. A reader of
#                concatenated values (JSON Lines, a stream) resumes at `offset`.
#   Depth        past `parser.MAX_DEPTH`. Not a syntax error: the document may
#                be perfectly good and simply deeper than this parser goes.
#   NumberRange  the literal is legal JSON but outside Dawn's Int/Float model
#                (RFC 8259 §6 leaves those limits to the implementation). It
#                carries the literal, so a caller that needs exact decimals can
#                keep the digits and re-read them itself.
#   Syntax       everything else: the byte at `offset` cannot be there.
##
## Why a `parse` failed, in the terms a caller can act on: input that ran out,
## a value followed by more input, nesting past `MAX_DEPTH`, a number outside
## Dawn's model (carrying its literal), or a plain syntax error.
pub type JsonErrorKind =
  | Syntax
  | Truncated
  | Trailing
  | Depth
  | NumberRange(literal: String)
derive Show

## What `parse` returns on failure: the kind, the code-point offset it was
## noticed at, and the sentence a human reads. `error_text` renders the last two
## in the shape the old `String` error had.
pub type JsonError = {
  kind: JsonErrorKind,
  offset: Int,
  message: String,
} derive Show

## A `JsonError` as the one-line message the `String` error used to be:
## `"unexpected character at offset 6"`. The migration from that error type is
## this call.
pub fn error_text(e: JsonError) -> String =
  e.message ++ " at offset " ++ to_string(e.offset)
